The attack by Sam Rainsy's supporters has left Kem Sokha's supporters unhappy and posted a flurry of attacks on social media, ranging from minor supporters to people close to Kem Sokha.
The Facebook page of a former CNRP supporter in France has expressed outrage that Sam Rainsy's convicts are indeed innocent and that Kem Sokha is always wrong.
The first phase of the Skills Development Program (SDP) supports vulnerable youth in earning income and employment opportunities, as well as contributing to the promotion of well-coordinated technical and vocational education, quality, relevance and environment. Across the country.
The second phase of the skills development program starts on July 1, 2020, with a total budget of 8.3 million Swiss francs for a four-year implementation plan. The overall goal of the Skills Development Program (SDP) is to increase decent employment and income opportunities for vulnerable youth and low-skilled staff.
Technical and Vocational Education and Training (TVET) is a priority area of Swiss international development cooperation as it is key to economic development and poverty reduction and as a means of imparting knowledge and skills relevant to men and women. Young women, including soft skills, to enter the job market with self-confidence as well as to meet the needs of the job market.
* First, the development of TVET training facilities: the development of the quality of provincial vocational training centers (PTCs) through the process of developing training institutions with cooperation and comprehensiveness.
* Second: TVET Innovation Lab: Promote innovative approaches to skills development and finding solutions to fundamental problems in the labor market in collaboration with the private sector and other service providers.
* Third: Training for vulnerable youth: Provide quality technical, soft and entrepreneurial skills training based on the labor market to poor and vulnerable youth and link them to paid or occupational work. By yourself.
* Fourth: Hospitality of Cambodia (HoKa): Provide hospitality training to low-skilled employees in the enterprise and strengthen the capacity of business owners and managers in the hospitality sector, as well as trainers of provincial and private tourism departments.
At the launching ceremony of the second phase of the Skills Development Program (SDP) on the morning of July 20, 2020 at the Seitel Phnom Penh Phokitra Hotel, Mr. Pech Sophoan, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training, said that the Skills Development Program Intervention from sub-national to national level to improve TVET system as well as increase the efficiency and relevance of training to training institutions.
He said the skills development program has built the capacity of tourism professionals, supported the generation of low-skilled staff and contributed to the development of tourism.
Mr. Pich Sophoan said that the Ministry of Tourism will continue to support skills development programs through its national and sub-national mechanisms to enhance the capacity of Cambodian tourism professionals and provide on-site and online evaluation and training opportunities. That can guarantee the re-skills and add skills to tourism professionals.
(Phnom Penh): For 5 consecutive days, from July 13-17, 2020, Cambodian veterans living in 6 communes in Russey Keo district received donations in the form of food and some money to solve their livelihood. Daily by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen through the working group of the Honorary President of Khan Veterans Association led by Mr. Chea Pisey and District Governor.
This series of activities, in addition to being led by the Honorary President of the Cambodian Veterans Association in Khan Russey Keo, was also attended by the Deputy Inspector of Khan Police, represented by Colonel Heang Tharet, District Police Inspector and President of the Khan Cambodian Veterans Association, Khan Special Military Region Representative, Including civil servants, police and commune authorities.
During the five consecutive days of humanitarian activities, Mr. Chea Pisey, Honorary President of the Cambodian Veterans Association of Russey Keo District and District Governor, emphasized, "And you, the veterans, are really valuable in sacrificing your life, both physically and mentally, with fresh flesh and blood, to bring peace to the next generation."
(Banteay Meanchey): At least 10 people in Kbal Koh and Poipet villages in Poipet commune, Banteay Meanchey province are suffering from a disease, falling ill and some abnormal fever until seizures.
However, the governor of Banteay Meanchey, Mr. Oum Rath, confirmed that the condition of some people have improved and others are still hospitalized under the care of doctors.
According to the governor of Banteay Meanchey province, until now, the medical team has not determined what kind of disease, but suspected that the disease.
According to the villagers, the victim had soft hands and feet, then a high fever and a rash on his body or red flesh. They also have seizures due to overheating.
Authorities and paramedics are on their way to the village to interrogate and rescue the villagers. Those with high fever were taken by ambulance to the hospital for treatment and follow-up to determine the patient's condition.
Dr. K. Piseth, director of Poipet 1 Health Center, who visited the scene, did not elaborate on the symptoms or how many patients there were, saying he was not sure.
Separately, an official from the Poipet City Executive District Office, Dr. Singh Sopheap, said by telephone that he did not know the number of patients sent from the two villages to the hospital because he had just arrived at the scene today. He said that the patients sent to Poipet Referral Hospital on July 20 were 4 males, 2 females, 2 but 3 had suspicious signs to be monitored, one was just normal fever.
Poipet commune council officials said that seven patients were taken to hospital on July 19, four of them girls, most of them children. Follow up on the patient's symptoms.
